course: dictionary
lesson: 2791
title: Disobedience: refusing to follow the rules
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disobedience is a noun. It means refusing or failing to do what a person in charge, a rule or a law tells you. Its root is the word obey, so disobedience is the opposite of obedience. Notice the clues in a sentence, like «punished for disobedience», which tells you that disobedience got someone into trouble.
explain: Civil disobedience is a more formal use. Here, a person breaks a law on purpose, but in a peaceful way, to protest that the law is unfair. Rosa Parks and Gandhi used civil disobedience in history. Their disobedience was not about being naughty; it was about trying to change something they believed was wrong.
explain: Disobedience can be good or bad, depending on the situation. «A guide dog stops at kerbs and steps so its blind owner knows they are there» shows intelligent disobedience. The dog refuses an order that would lead its owner into danger. Ask yourself: who is giving the order, and would following it be safe and fair?
fact: Disobedience means refusing or failing to do what someone in charge, a rule or a law tells you.
fact: Obey is the root of disobedience, so the two words are opposites.
fact: «Civil disobedience» means breaking a law peacefully on purpose to protest that it is unfair.
fact: Rosa Parks and Gandhi used civil disobedience in history.
fact: «Punished for disobedience» means someone got into trouble for not following a rule.
exercise: «The class was punished for disobedience when they ran in the corridor.» What does disobedience mean here? | Running very fast | Being very kind to others | *Refusing to follow a rule | Winning a race
exercise: Rosa Parks and Gandhi used civil disobedience. What is civil disobedience? | Shouting in a library by mistake | Breaking a plate by accident | Obeying every law without question | *Breaking a law peacefully on purpose to protest that it is unfair
exercise: A guide dog refuses an order that would lead its owner into danger. What kind of disobedience is this? | *Intelligent disobedience | Sleepy disobedience | Lazy disobedience | Silent disobedience
test: «Christians call the first disobedience the Fall.» What does the Fall mean? | *The moment sin and trouble came into the world | The day Adam and Eve got a new garden | A sunny day in the garden | A time when everyone went to sleep
test: Why might someone use civil disobedience? | To get out of doing homework | To make a loud noise on purpose | To win a sports prize | *To protest that a law is unfair
exam: Which words best describe disobedience? | Asking questions politely | Doing exactly what your teacher says | *Refusing to do what a rule or person in charge tells you | Being very quick at your work
